{"id": "721b5ef3-4aee-4c0a-91ff-91a67795c849", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll scan IAM across your associated AWS accounts for Bedrock long-term API keys, which are service-specific credentials for Bedrock on IAM users. I'll check each key's age, expiry, status, permissions and recent usage, then recommend which to remove.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_X8Ze0SIbPjKffcBxjFU0pC", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-api-key-audit", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["investigating-infrastructure-changes", "verifying-with-docs"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Produce a complete inventory and risk audit of every long-term Amazon Bedrock API key across ALL AWS accounts associated with this agent space (use list_associations to find them; the agent space's own account is 012345678901_1). READ-ONLY: do not modify or delete anything.",
  "context": "Long-term Bedrock API keys are IAM service-specific credentials with service name bedrock.amazonaws.com, attached to IAM users (the console-created ones usually live on users named 'BedrockAPIKey-xxxx' but check ALL IAM users, since keys can be created on any user). The user wants a full report plus a recommendation on which keys to remove. Short-term keys (presigned, up to 12h) are NOT in scope.",
  "resources": ["All associated AWS accounts, IAM (global), CloudTrail (us-east-1 plus any region where Bedrock calls are seen)"],
  "timeframe": "Usage lookback: last 90 days up to 2026-11-24T18:52:00Z",
  "criteria": "For each account: enumerate all IAM users and call list-service-specific-credentials filtered to service-name bedrock.amazonaws.com. For each key capture: account ID, IAM user name, credential ID, alias if present, status (Active/Inactive/Expired), create date, age in days, expiration date (or 'never expires'), whether the user has console password / other access keys / tags (owner tag), attached managed + inline + group policies (flag AdministratorAccess, bedrock:*, wildcards, or anything beyond Bedrock invoke, e.g. AmazonBedrockLimitedAccess/FullAccess), and last observed use (look in CloudTrail via the lookup tool for Bedrock API calls by that IAM user; state clearly if usage can't be determined). Do NOT list or print the secret key value. Flag risks: never expires, older than 90 days, inactive/expired but not cleaned up, never used or unused in 30+/90 days, over-broad permissions, no owner tag, user also has other credentials, multiple keys per user. Finish with: per-account counts, a table of all keys, and a classification of each key as REMOVE / REVIEW / KEEP with a one-line reason, ordered by priority. Note any accounts you couldn't access or checks you couldn't complete. Optionally confirm with AWS docs the current guidance on long-term vs short-term Bedrock API keys."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit long-term Bedrock API keys across associated AWS accounts"}], "created_at": null}

